      "hints": [
        "Undo the natural logarithm first by exponentiating both sides.",
        "From $1+e^{2y}=e^x$, isolate $e^{2y}$.",
        "Take natural logarithms again and divide by $2$ to make $y$ the subject; note that real values require $e^x-1>0$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Start with the given equation:\n$$\\ln(1 + \\mathrm{e}^{2y}) = x$$",
        "Exponentiate both sides with base $\\mathrm{e}$:\n$$1 + \\mathrm{e}^{2y} = \\mathrm{e}^x$$",
        "Subtract $1$ from both sides to isolate the exponential term in $y$:\n$$\\mathrm{e}^{2y} = \\mathrm{e}^x - 1$$",
        "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n$$2y = \\ln(\\mathrm{e}^x - 1)$$",
        "Divide by $2$ to express $y$ in terms of $x$:\n$$y = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln(\\mathrm{e}^x - 1)$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
